(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 17-year-old basketball player collapses briefly during a game and recovers. In the emergency department he is in sinus rhythm at 78. His electrocardiogram shows a PR interval of 0.10 seconds and a delta wave (a slurred upstroke at the start of the QRS) with a widened QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sick sinus syndrome","Focal atrial tachycardia","Wolff-Parkinson-White","Atrioventricular nodal reentry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A short PR interval with a delta wave and widened QRS in sinus rhythm is pre-excitation from an accessory pathway (the bundle of Kent): Wolff-Parkinson-White, which classically first shows itself as collapse in a young athlete. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 19-year-old swimmer has an electrocardiogram before a sports physical. She has no symptoms. The rate is 72, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS, but the beats come closer together as she breathes in and spread apart as she breathes out. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Sick sinus syndrome","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal P wave before every narrow QRS at 60 to 100, with the rate rising on inspiration and falling on expiration, is sinus arrhythmia: a normal, physiologic rhythm that is irregular with a pattern. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a heart rate of 122 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. His temperature is 39.2°C and he has a productive cough and crackles at the right base. He is alert with a blood pressure of 126/78. Which is the best treatment for his heart rate?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Treat the infection and fever","Adenosine","Vagal maneuver"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A sinus tachycardia from fever and infection is an appropriate response to a pathologic process, and appropriate sinus tachycardia is managed by treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has episodes of lightheadedness and one faint. A 24-hour ambulatory monitor shows runs of a fast, irregular rhythm that alternate with slow rates, and a long pause each time the fast rhythm stops. Which fast rhythm is most likely on her monitor?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fast rhythms alternating with bradycardia and offset pauses are tachy-brady syndrome, a form of sick sinus syndrome in which the tachyarrhythmia is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation at 170 has a blood pressure of 74/40 and is drowsy and confused. Which is the most appropriate immediate step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous rate control","Oral rate control","Anticoagulate for 21 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable atrial fibrillation (hypotension and altered mental status) is treated with synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has focal atrial tachycardia at 165. He is hemodynamically stable but symptomatic with palpitations. Which acute treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous beta blocker","Intravenous atropine","Unsynchronized defibrillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia is treated with an intravenous beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker while the underlying cause is identified and treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has months of fatigue and two near-faints. Repeated electrocardiograms show sinus bradycardia at 42, and her symptoms match the slow rate. She takes no rate-slowing drugs and her thyroid tests are normal. Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Permanent pacemaker","Atropine as needed","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic, symptomatic sinus bradycardia with no reversible cause, as here in an older adult, is managed with a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has inappropriate sinus tachycardia at about 110 after a full workup excluded other causes. Her only symptom is awareness of her heartbeat, and she asks whether she needs medication. Which response is most accurate?","choices":["Usually left untreated; benign","She needs a lifelong beta blocker","Cardioversion will cure it","She needs anticoagulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is generally left untreated: patients tolerate the symptoms better than the treatment, beta blockers are poorly tolerated, and the prognosis is usually benign. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with atrial fibrillation, a prior major bleed and heavy alcohol use is being considered for anticoagulation. Which tool estimates his risk of major bleeding on anticoagulation?","choices":["HAS-BLED (bleeding risk) score","CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score","Wells (clot likelihood) score","Duke (endocarditis) criteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The HAS-BLED (bleeding risk) score estimates the risk of major bleeding for patients on anticoagulation, to weigh risk against benefit in atrial fibrillation care. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man's frequent premature atrial contractions are shown on monitoring to trigger his episodes of atrial fibrillation. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Carotid endarterectomy","Septal myectomy","Catheter ablation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation is used if premature atrial contractions are frequent or are triggering atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man with Wolff-Parkinson-White asks which rhythm would be most dangerous for him. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Premature atrial contractions","Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial fibrillation down the pathway","Sinus tachycardia with exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation conducting down an accessory pathway is the most dangerous combination: it can exceed 200, approaching 300, and cause collapse. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has a stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 150 in which P waves cannot be clearly seen. Adenosine is given. Besides possibly ending the rhythm, what can it reveal?","choices":["ST elevation from infarction","A prolonged QT interval","A bundle branch block","Whether P waves are present"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine may convert a supraventricular tachycardia to normal sinus rhythm, or at least slow the rate so you can see whether P waves are there. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has had atrial fibrillation for years and asks why her clinician insists on a blood thinner. Which complication is the anticoagulant meant to prevent?","choices":["Stroke from a clot","Heart block","Ventricular fibrillation","A faster heart rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation in atrial fibrillation prevents stroke; the CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score is used to assess that risk, which rises with age. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man in atrial flutter has a heart rate of 150, a blood pressure of 70/40, and is confused. Which is the next step?","choices":["Vagal maneuver","Synchronized cardioversion","Oral rate control","Anticoagulate for 21 days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable atrial flutter (hypotension with confusion) is treated with synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man is evaluated after a regular tachycardia at 220 that was terminated in the emergency department. An accessory pathway is suspected, but the tachycardia tracing shows no delta wave (slurred upstroke). On which tracing is pre-excitation (a short PR interval with a delta wave) most likely to be seen?","choices":["A tracing in sinus rhythm","A tracing during tachycardia","A chest radiograph","An echocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Evidence of pre-excitation (short PR interval and delta wave) is found on electrocardiograms taken during normal sinus rhythm; during orthodromic tachycardia the QRS is narrow with no delta wave. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman with Wolff-Parkinson-White has had three emergency visits for reentrant tachycardia this year. Which long-term management is first line?","choices":["Lifelong anticoagulation","Ablation of the pathway","Lifelong oral adenosine","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation of the accessory pathway is first-line chronic management of Wolff-Parkinson-White, removing the substrate for reentry and pre-excited atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with hypertension and diabetes has nonvalvular atrial fibrillation and a high CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score. Which anticoagulant class is preferred?","choices":["Aspirin alone","None, given her age","A direct oral anticoagulant","Warfarin as first choice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Direct oral anticoagulants (dabigatran, apixaban, edoxaban or rivaroxaban) are preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation; a high stroke-risk score means she needs anticoagulation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with recurrent atrioventricular nodal reentry has been taught to stop episodes at home with a vagal maneuver. Tonight an episode stopped within minutes after she did so, and she now feels well. What is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["No urgent care is usually needed","Come to the emergency department","Start an anticoagulant","Arrange a pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If a vagal maneuver terminates nodal reentry in a patient who has been educated, urgent medical attention is usually not required. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 182 with palpitations and mild anxiety. Her blood pressure is 128/80, oxygen saturation 98%, and she is alert without chest pain. Which treatment should be tried first?","choices":["Adenosine","Intravenous amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Vagal maneuver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated first with a vagal maneuver, then adenosine (up to twice), keeping synchronized cardioversion for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has recurrent atrioventricular nodal reentry episodes that tend to start after coffee, alcohol or decongestant (sympathomimetic) use. She has no heart disease. Which counseling is most appropriate?","choices":["Start an anticoagulant","Expect a pacemaker","Limit caffeine and alcohol","Avoid all exercise"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nodal reentry may occur spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine, alcohol, beta-agonists and sympathomimetics, so limiting her known triggers is sensible counseling. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with hypertension, diabetes and obstructive sleep apnea has had palpitations for two days. His pulse is irregularly irregular at 132, and the electrocardiogram shows no P waves and a narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An irregularly irregular narrow complex rhythm with absent P waves is atrial fibrillation, and hypertension, diabetes, sleep apnea and older age are listed risk factors. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has had three recurrences of atrial flutter, and rate control has been hard to achieve. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Septal myectomy","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ablation is the definitive management of atrial flutter, which has a substantial recurrence rate and is harder to rate-control than fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man collapses while playing basketball and arrives with a very fast regular tachycardia. He is alert with a blood pressure of 112/70. A vagal maneuver fails, and adenosine slows the rate to reveal an irregular rhythm with wide QRS complexes, each with a delta wave (slurred upstroke). Which is the next step?","choices":["Oral metoprolol","Intravenous procainamide","A second dose of adenosine","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When slowing from adenosine reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is never repeated: nodal blockade leaves the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has recurrent, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia despite an oral beta blocker. She wants a definitive option. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Repeated adenosine","Lifelong anticoagulation","Catheter ablation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation of the ectopic focus is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia (about 90%), after oral beta blockers or calcium channel blockers as initial therapy. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has sudden palpitations after three energy drinks. She notices her neck throbbing, and examination shows a frog sign (rapid visible pulsations in the neck). Her heart rate is 172 and regular, with a narrow QRS and no visible P waves. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A paroxysmal, regular narrow tachycardia with hidden P waves, neck pulsations (the frog sign) and a stimulant trigger in a young woman without heart disease is atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has occasional premature junctional contractions (early narrow beats with no visible P wave) on a monitor and is anxious about them. He has no symptoms and no heart disease. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance; no treatment","Metoprolol for life"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No treatment is needed if premature junctional contractions are asymptomatic and infrequent, and reassurance of the benign nature of the rhythm is often adequate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is brought in confused, with a blood pressure of 76/40. The monitor shows a slow, regular rhythm at 36 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Permanent pacemaker","Atropine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia is treated first with atropine; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows, and chronic symptomatic disease gets a permanent one. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with an exacerbation and respiratory failure. His pulse is 128 and irregular. The electrocardiogram shows a P wave before every QRS, with at least three different P-wave shapes in lead II. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Focal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter, variable block","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irregular tachycardia with a P wave for every QRS and at least three distinct P-wave shapes in one lead is multifocal atrial tachycardia, usually from severe lung disease or heart failure in an acutely ill patient. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has atrial flutter (sawtooth flutter waves) with a ventricular rate of 140. She is alert, with a blood pressure of 124/78 and no chest pain. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Adenosine twice","Permanent pacemaker","A beta or calcium channel blocker","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable atrial flutter is rate-controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker; rate control is the first goal, though harder to achieve than in fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with sick sinus syndrome has pauses on monitoring that alternate with runs of ventricular tachycardia. Which device is indicated?","choices":["Ablation alone","Pacemaker-defibrillator","Pacemaker alone","No device; observe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tachy-brady syndrome often needs a permanent pacemaker, and when the tachyarrhythmia is ventricular tachycardia the patient needs a pacemaker with an implantable cardioverter defibrillator. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
